================================================================================
 ABI v1 SECTION 9: THE THREE DELIVERIES AT THE FLOOR, AND THE A/B/C RPC GRID
 With two corrections: there is no loopback-TCP penalty, and the codec is the
 majority of a real RPC's CPU.
================================================================================

CONFIGURATION (R7, R13, R14)
  rustc 1.94.1 (e408947bf 2026-03-25), release, PIE
  incumbent  prost 0.14.4 + tonic 0.14.6, the codec `packages/rust` reaches
             through gRPC's generated marshaller (R14)
  linkage    shared library (ak-core as a cdylib), confirmed from ldd and not
             from the build log
  machine    4 vCPU Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz, shared container
  crossing   1.8 ns forward and 1.8 ns forward+reverse (R13, this box;
             logs/rust/stage2-four-arms-M1.log, 20,000,000 crossings)
  payload    P2.2, 540,422 B -- the shape the control plane actually moves
  transport  Unix domain socket PRIMARY, loopback TCP as a labelled second row
  pinned     stream window 4 MiB, CONNECTION window 4 MiB, max message 2 MiB,
             adaptive window OFF, client Nagle at tonic's default (nodelay on).
             ArmoniK's settings, applied to BOTH the tonic arm and the core arms.
             `armonik-transport` reads `GrpcClient__TcpNagleAlgorithm`, defaults
             it false and applies `set_nodelay(!nagle)`, so ArmoniK ships Nagle
             OFF and the CLIENT was never the problem here.
  rounds     7 x 96 calls, median; CPU per RPC is the headline, wall beside it
  script     poc/rust/gen/rpcgrid.sh

WHAT WAS ASKED FOR, AND WHAT CAME BACK
  Asked: price section 9's three deliveries AT THE FLOOR, so that a managed
  slice's delivery table has a control; and build the RPC arm as an A/B/C grid.
  Both are below and both came back as expected.  Two things that were NOT asked
  for came back with them, and they are the larger results.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1. THE FLOOR: THE THREE DELIVERIES ARE INDISTINGUISHABLE, BY CONSTRUCTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  blocking  ak_call_unary        2 forward, 0 reverse
  callback  ak_call_unary_cb     2 forward, 1 reverse
  queue     ak_call_unary_q      3 forward, 0 reverse

  The three differ by exactly ONE crossing.  On this host a crossing is 1.8 ns
  and an RPC is 1.4 to 2.2 ms of CPU, so the delivery choice is 0.0001% of the
  call -- roughly six orders of magnitude below the 0% to 71% spread this
  harness can resolve at all.

  So the measured answer ("indistinguishable", every ratio 1.000 to 1.2 and
  unsigned) is worth much less than the reason for it.  **No run of this harness
  could ever have shown a delivery difference.**  That is what makes it a
  control rather than a result.

  WHAT THIS LICENSES A MANAGED SLICE TO CONCLUDE.  If Java or C# measures its
  three deliveries apart by a margin it can resolve, that margin is not the
  delivery shape -- there is nothing in the delivery shape to measure.  It is
  that host's price for the ONE crossing that differs, plus whatever its runtime
  wraps around a callback (an upcall stub, a pinned object, a thread transition)
  or around a queue (a wait, a handoff).  The Java slice prices an upcall at
  about 80 ns, some 44x this slice's crossing, and callback is precisely the
  delivery that takes a reverse crossing per call.  This table is the evidence
  that there is nothing else in the ABI for such a margin to be.

  The crossing counts are read from the code, not from a counting build: the
  core's counters live in the encode and decode contexts and the RPC half has
  neither.  What makes them trustworthy is the property section 9 rests on --
  neither `crates/rpc` nor ak-core's rpc module mentions a message type, so
  there is nowhere a per-field cost could enter.  `gen/rpcgrid.sh` step 1 checks
  that mechanically rather than asserting it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 2. THE GRID: A, B AND C ARE WITHIN THE NOISE OF EACH OTHER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  A  prost + tonic     the incumbent stack (R14)
  B  prost + core      prost's codec, the core's transport
  C  core + core       both halves native, behind the C ABI

  Every cell lands between 0.933 and 1.064 of A, on both transports, at every
  flight -- which is this harness's noise.  Two readings, and the second is the
  one that matters:

  B - A IS NOT A TRANSPORT COMPARISON IN THIS SLICE.  Both transports are tonic,
  so what B - A isolates here is the cost of putting the C ABI in front of one
  transport, with the transport difference removed by construction.  It is about
  zero.  For a managed host the same subtraction is grpc-java or grpc-dotnet
  against tonic, which is a DIFFERENT QUANTITY and must not be read against this
  row.  C - B is the codec with the transport held constant, and is also zero --
  unsurprising, since prost is already native and stages 1 to 3 put the core
  within a few percent of it on these shapes.

  The honest summary of the grid on its own: on a host where the incumbent codec
  is already native, wrapping the stack in the C ABI costs nothing measurable,
  and swapping the codec gains nothing measurable.  Neither half of that is a
  claim about a managed host.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 3. NOT ASKED FOR: THE CODEC IS THE MAJORITY OF A REAL RPC'S CPU
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The deliveries table and the grid move THE SAME BYTES over THE SAME CALL: the
  server's fixed response is prost's encoding of P2.2, the deliveries arm sends
  those bytes and receives them back touching no codec at all, and cell A encodes
  that same value and decodes that same response every call.  So A minus blocking
  is exactly one encode plus one decode, with transport, server, windows, flight
  and machine identical on both sides of the minus sign.

      flight   opaque us/RPC   A us/RPC   codec us/RPC   codec %
      UDS   1          1666.7     4687.5         3020.8     64.4%
      UDS   8          1979.2     4791.7         2812.5     58.7%
      UDS  16          2083.3     5104.2         3020.8     59.2%
      TCP   1          1458.3     4583.3         3125.0     68.2%
      TCP   8          1770.8     4687.5         2916.7     62.2%
      TCP  16          1979.2     4895.8         2916.7     59.6%

  Between 59% and 68% of the CPU of a real gRPC call, about 60% away from the
  flight-1 rows where the denominator is smallest, at the payload the control plane
  actually moves, is the codec.  That is the premise the whole exploration rests
  on and it had not been measured end to end anywhere in this branch: stages 1
  to 3 timed the codec in isolation, stage 4 timed the RPC with no codec in the
  loop, and nobody had put the two on the same axis.

  TWO THINGS IT DOES NOT SAY.  It does not say a native codec recovers that
  share: cells A, B and C are indistinguishable here, so on THIS host there is
  nothing to recover.  The share is what a managed host's codec has to compete
  for, not a saving anyone has banked.  And it is a share of CPU against a
  loopback with a do-nothing server, which is as small as a real deployment's
  denominator ever gets -- so it is an UPPER bound on the fraction, not a
  typical one.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 4. NOT ASKED FOR: THERE IS NO LOOPBACK-TCP PENALTY, AND STAGE 4'S STATED
    HAZARD IS WRONG
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  `README.md` R9 attributes the order-of-magnitude gap between CPU and wall at
  flight 1 to HTTP/2 flow control: "a 540 KB response does not fit the
  65,535-octet initial stream window, so a single call in flight spends most of
  its wall clock idle waiting for `WINDOW_UPDATE`", and it names this slice's own
  33 ms per call as the case in point.  (The attribution is R9 in README.md;
  `stage4-rpc.log` carries the 33 ms figures but states no mechanism.)  That
  explanation is wrong, and this run refutes it in three steps, each of which is
  a row in the window-control table.

  (a) PINNING THE WINDOWS CHANGES ALMOST NOTHING.  ArmoniK's 4 MiB stream AND
      connection windows move the wall column by a few percent on either
      transport.  (Pinning them at all required a new entry point: `ak_client_new`
      takes only a URI, so before `ak_client_new_opts` the core could not express
      ArmoniK's transport at all, and every RPC figure in this branch is a
      64 KiB-window figure.)

  (b) THE SAME 540 KB IS 2 ms OVER UDS AND 30 ms OVER LOOPBACK TCP.  HTTP/2 flow
      control is a property of the protocol and is identical on both transports.
      A transport-independent cause cannot produce a transport-dependent result.

  (c) 1 KB COSTS MORE THAN 540 KB OVER TCP -- 44 ms against 30 ms.  So what is
      left is per-CALL, not per-BYTE, and a per-call cost of tens of milliseconds
      is not flow control at any window size.  Note the DIRECTION, which is the
      sharper half of this: if flow control drove the wall clock, the 540 KB
      response (which needs the WINDOW_UPDATE round trips) would cost MORE than
      the 1 KB one (which needs none).  It costs less.  That is backwards for
      flow control and exactly right for Nagle, where a large response has full
      segments to send and never waits while a small one is a lone short write.

  AND HERE IS WHAT IT ACTUALLY IS.  `crates/rpc` drives tonic through
  `serve_with_incoming`, and tonic documents that the builder's `tcp_nodelay`
  (which defaults to TRUE) is IGNORED on that path; `TcpIncoming::from(listener)`
  leaves its own nodelay unset, so `set_accepted_socket_options` never touches
  the socket.  The server end kept Nagle ON while tonic's client had it off by
  default.  A gRPC response is HEADERS, then DATA, then TRAILERS; with Nagle on
  the writer the second small write waits for the peer's ACK of the first, and
  Linux's delayed-ACK timer is 40 ms.  44 ms for a 1 KB response is that timer
  with the round trip on top.

  ONE SOCKET OPTION, NOTHING ELSE CHANGED:

      transport  server socket          CPU us/RPC   wall us/RPC
      UDS        -                          2708.3        2081.9
      TCP        TCP_NODELAY (`serve`)      2500.0        2007.8
      TCP        Nagle (`serve_nagle`)      3541.7       31585.6
      UDS        -                           208.3         151.3   1 KB
      TCP        TCP_NODELAY (`serve`)       104.2         141.8   1 KB
      TCP        Nagle (`serve_nagle`)       729.2       44039.6   1 KB

  (All six are default-client rows from section 6's window-control table; the
  pinned rows are alongside them there and differ by a few percent.)

  With the option set, loopback TCP and UDS agree on both payloads, on both
  columns.  **There was never a transport gap.**  Every loopback-TCP wall figure
  in this branch, stage 4's included, is measuring a harness defect, and any
  slice that reported UDS as faster than TCP reported this and not a transport.

  AND THE CPU COLUMN MOVED TOO, WHICH MATTERS MORE THAN THE WALL COLUMN DID.
  Every slice has been told CPU is the trustworthy column.  Against the old
  server that was only half true.  Two rows of one table cannot establish it on a
  shared box, so section 6 interleaves them -- nagle, nodelay, nagle, nodelay,
  five adjacent pairs, so drift moves both members of a pair together and cannot
  manufacture a consistent sign:

      pair   nagle CPU   nodelay CPU   CPU x     nagle wall   nodelay wall  wall x
         1      3333.3        2395.8   1.391x       29957.9         1863.2   16.1x
         2      3229.2        2500.0   1.292x       31493.2         1935.6   16.3x
         3      3541.7        2812.5   1.259x       29055.5         2215.9   13.1x
         4      3854.2        2604.2   1.480x       29624.4         2074.8   14.3x
         5      3645.8        2812.5   1.296x       28189.5         2273.1   12.4x
       med                             1.296x                                14.3x

  **Five pairs out of five, same sign, median 1.296x.**  Nagle on the server
  inflated the loopback-TCP CPU column by about 30%.  The mechanism is the same
  one: a stalled write parks the reactor and a timer wakes it, so the call costs
  extra epoll and scheduler work at both ends -- and both ends are in this
  process, so both land in its utime+stime.

  WHAT THAT COSTS THE BRANCH, precisely.  A loopback-TCP CPU figure taken against
  the old server is INFLATED, not merely accompanied by an unreadable wall
  figure.  What it does NOT cost is a RATIO between two arms that both went
  through the same server: stage 4's `CPU/tonic` column is such a ratio and still
  stands, while its absolute `CPU us/RPC` column does not.  Any slice quoting an
  absolute loopback-TCP CPU number from before the flip should re-take it; any
  slice quoting a ratio need not.

  WHAT SURVIVES OF R9.  Its conclusion does -- "measure CPU, not wall clock" is
  now better supported, not worse, and so is "wall-clock columns are not
  comparable across slices unless the configuration is stated".  What is refuted
  is the MECHANISM it names and the inference a reader draws from it: that a
  slice showing a large wall/CPU gap on P2.2 has a window to raise.  Raising the
  window here bought a few percent; one socket option bought 14x to 300x.  R9 and
  README.md belong to the aggregating session, so this is reported, not edited.

  AND ONE MORE THING IT COSTS.  Three slices have now spent effort pinning
  windows to chase this gap, and `design/SHAPES.md` carries a whole subsection on
  per-stack initial window and auto-tuning defaults.  That subsection is still
  correct and still worth having for a real deployment; it is just not what the
  loopback numbers were measuring.

  WHAT WAS CHANGED, AND BY WHOM.  This slice ADDED `rpc::serve_nodelay` beside
  `rpc::serve` and left `rpc::serve` byte-for-byte as it was, because R0 makes a
  change to existing core behaviour the aggregating session's call.  It took the
  flip: `rpc::serve` now sets the option, `rpc::serve_nagle` preserves the old
  form so the artifact above stays reproducible, and `serve_nodelay` is a
  deprecated alias.  This log's tables are re-taken against the flipped
  `rpc::serve`; the window-control rows above reach the old behaviour through
  `serve_nagle` and are where the artifact is kept as evidence.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 5. HAZARDS (R9), AND THEY ARE LARGE HERE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - FOUR vCPUs, with the server's two worker threads, each arm's runtime and N
    host threads all on them.  At 8 and 16 in flight the machine is
    oversubscribed before the measurement starts.  Those rows bound the arms'
    RELATIVE behaviour under identical oversubscription and nothing else.
  - One row was visibly a casualty of that on an earlier run of this same binary:
    cell C at TCP flight 16 read 1.435 of A (6875 us against 4792).  It is the
    MEDIAN of seven rounds, so it was sustained contention rather than one bad
    round.  It did not reproduce -- the run in section 6 has it at 1.082 -- and
    it is recorded here rather than quietly dropped.  It is not a finding, and it
    is the reason the flight 8 and 16 rows carry no conclusions.
  - The floor table's own spread column shows the same thing from the other side:
    at flight 1 it reaches 71% between three arms that differ by 1.8 ns.  A
    harness with a 71% spread is not measuring the arms; that is the point of
    quoting it.
  - The server is in the same process and contends for the same four vCPUs, so
    server cost is inside every number in every cell equally.
  - CPU is process utime+stime at 10 ms resolution.  The ROUND carries it and the
    per-call figure is a division, not a per-call measurement.  That is why the
    floor table quotes a spread rather than an error bar.
  - No TLS, no retry, no metadata, no deadlines, no streaming, no failure
    injection, no server-side measurement.  The RPC half's case is BEHAVIOURAL
    and none of that behaviour is exercised here.
  - Cancellation: `ak_call_cancel` exists and nothing here calls it.
  - `design/SHAPES.md`'s carrier-thread row stays empty: Rust has no carrier
    thread to pin, and R8 says report it empty rather than substitute.
  - Absolutes do not travel (R13).  What travels is the crossing counts, the
    ratios inside one process, and the two corrections in sections 3 and 4.

