# the floor, TCP: what the delivery choice costs when a crossing is free
#
#  flight    min us/RPC     spread us    spread %  1 crossing %
#       1        1458.3         416.7      28.57%     0.000123%
#       8        1770.8         416.7      23.53%     0.000102%
#      16        1979.2         104.2       5.26%     0.000091%
#
# Read the last two columns against each other. The ABI difference between the
# three deliveries is one crossing, which is about six orders of magnitude below
# the spread this harness can resolve. **So on this host the three deliveries are
# indistinguishable, and they are indistinguishable BY CONSTRUCTION, not by
# luck**: the spread column is the harness's own noise, and no run of it could
# ever have shown a delivery difference. That is what makes it a control.
#
# WHAT THIS LICENSES A MANAGED SLICE TO CONCLUDE, and what it does not.
# If Java or C# measures its three deliveries apart by a resolvable margin, the
# margin is NOT the delivery shape -- it is that host's cost for the ONE crossing
# that differs, plus whatever its runtime wraps around a callback (an upcall
# stub, a pinned object, a thread transition) or around a queue (a wait, a
# handoff). The Java slice already prices an upcall at about 80 ns, some 44x this
# slice's crossing, and the callback delivery is the one that takes a reverse
# crossing per call. That is the quantity to attribute it to, and this table is
# the evidence that there is nothing else in the ABI for it to be.

